The `:has()` pseudo-class can be used to select a parent element that contains a specific child [1]. The example provided demonstrates how to style a `<section>` element when it contains a child with the `featured` class. Here is the relevant code snippet:

```css
section:has(.featured) {
  border: 2px solid blue;
}
```

This CSS will apply a blue border to any `<section>` that contains an element with the class `featured`.

To add state to a React component, you can use either a class component with the `state` property or the `useState` hook. Here are examples of both methods:

### Using Class Component
```js
import { Component } from 'react';

export default class Counter extends Component {
  state = {
    name: 'Taylor',
    age: 42,
  };

  handleNameChange = (e) => {
    this.setState({
      name: e.target.value
    });
  }

  handleAgeChange = () => {
    this.setState({
      age: this.state.age + 1
    });
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <>
        <input
          value={this.state.name}
          onChange={this.handleNameChange}
        />
        <button onClick={this.handleAgeChange}>
          Increment age
        </button>
        <p>Hello, {this.state.name}. You are {this.state.age}.</p>
      </>
    );
  }
}
```

### Using `useState` Hook
```js
import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Form() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('Taylor');
  const [age, setAge] = useState(42);

  return (
    <>
      <input
        value={name}
        onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)}
      />
      <button onClick={() => setAge(age + 1)}>
        Increment age
      </button>
      <p>Hello, {name}. You are {age}.</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

Both methods allow you to manage state within your component. The class component approach is used in React versions prior to 16.8, while the `useState` hook is available from React 16.8 onwards and is recommended for new projects due to its simplicity and reusability.

The Vue Composition API allows you to declare computed properties and other reactive state within a component. Here is a basic example of how to declare a computed property using the Composition API in a Vue 3 component:

```vue
<script setup>
import { ref, computed } from 'vue'

// reactive state
const count = ref(0)

// computed property
const doubleCount = computed(() => count.value * 2)

// functions that mutate state and trigger updates
function increment() {
  count.value++
}

// lifecycle hooks
onMounted(() => {
  console.log(`The initial count is ${count.value}.`)
})
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    Count is: {{ count }}
    Double count is: {{ doubleCount }}
  </div>
  <button @click="increment">Increment</button>
</template>
```

In this example, `doubleCount` is a computed property that returns `count.value * 2`. This property will automatically update whenever `count` changes, reflecting the reactive nature of the Composition API.

To create a dynamic route segment in the Next.js App Router, you define a segment in your route that captures a value from the URL. This is done by placing square brackets `[]` around the segment name. For example, in the route `app/blog/[slug]/page.js`, `[slug]` is a dynamic segment that captures the value from the URL path.

When a user navigates to a URL like `/blog/a`, Next.js will pass the captured value `{ slug: 'a' }` to your page via the `params` prop. Here is a short code example:

```javascript
// app/blog/[slug]/page.js
export default function BlogPostPage({ params }) {
  return <div>Blog Post: {params.slug}</div>;
}
```

In this example, `params.slug` will be `'a'` when the URL is `/blog/a`.

Dynamic segments are often referred to as path params, route params, or URL params [1]. They are passed as the `params` prop to `layout`, `page`, `route`, and `generateMetadata` functions [2].

The useState composable in Nuxt creates a reactive and SSR-friendly shared state [1][2]. It is an SSR-friendly `ref` replacement, meaning its value will be preserved after server-side rendering and shared across all components using a unique key [2][3]. The data inside `useState` must be serializable to JSON, so it should not contain classes, functions, or symbols [2].

Here is an example of how to use the `useState` composable:

```ts
// app/composables/useFoo.ts
export const useFoo = () => {
  return useState('foo', () => 'bar')
}
```

Or using the default export:

```ts
// app/composables/use-foo.ts
export default function () {
  return useState('foo', () => 'bar')
}
```

In a component, you can then import and use it like this:

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
const foo = useFoo()
</script>

<template>
  <div>
    {{ foo }}
  </div>
</template>
```

A union type in TypeScript is a type formed from two or more other types, representing values that may be any one of those types [2]. For example, `number | string` represents a value that can be either a number or a string [2][3].

To work with a value of a union type, you need to narrow the union with code to determine which specific type the value is. This can be done using type guards like `typeof` or `Array.isArray` [3][4]. For instance, in the function `printId(id: number | string)`, you can narrow the union by checking the type of `id`:

```ts
function printId(id: number | string) {
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    // In this branch, id is of type 'string'
    console.log(id.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    // Here, id is of type 'number'
    console.log(id);
  }
}
```

If every member in a union has a property in common, you can use that property without narrowing. For example, both `string` and `string[]` have a `slice` method, so you can use it directly on a union type like `string | string[]` [3]:

```ts
function getFirstThree(x: string[] | string) {
  return x.slice(0, 3);
}
```

In this case, TypeScript infers the return type as `string[] | string` [3].

A Next.js App Routerban adatok lekérése a `fetch` függvény használatával történik. Ez a `fetch` nem a böngésző HTTP cache-jével foglalkozik, hanem a Next.js egyéni persistens cache-jével. A következő kód példa arra, hogyan lehet adatokat lekérni:

```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  let data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog');
  let posts = await data.json();
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

Ebben a példában a `fetch` függvény aszinkronan hívódik meg, és a visszatérő adatok JSON formátumban konvertálva visszatér a `posts` változóba.

To fetch data on the server in Next.js and Nuxt, you can use different methods as described below:

### Next.js
In Next.js, you can use the `fetch` API to make server-side requests. The `fetch` function in Next.js allows you to set caching and revalidation semantics for server-side requests. Here is an example of fetching data in a Next.js component:

```tsx
export default async function Page() {
  let data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog')
  let posts = await data.json()
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

### Nuxt
In Nuxt, you can use the `$fetch` helper for making HTTP requests. The `$fetch` helper is globally available in your Vue app or API routes. It is particularly useful for server-side rendering as it can emulate requests during server-side rendering, saving an additional API call. Here is an example of fetching data in a Nuxt component:

```ts
<script setup lang="ts">
const { data } = await useAsyncData('count', (_nuxtApp, { signal }) => $fetch('/api/count', { signal }))
</script>
```

```ts
// server/api/count.ts
export default defineEventHandler((event) => {
  return { count: 1 }
})
```

### Differences
- **Caching and Revalidation**: In Next.js, the `fetch` function allows you to set caching and revalidation semantics for server-side requests. In Nuxt, the `$fetch` helper is used for making HTTP requests, but it does not directly provide caching and revalidation options like Next.js.
- **Server-Side Rendering**: Nuxt's `$fetch` can emulate requests during server-side rendering, which can save an additional API call. In Next.js, you can set caching and revalidation semantics, but it does not have the same feature as Nuxt's `$fetch` for server-side rendering emulation.
- **State Transfer**: When using `$fetch` in Nuxt components without wrapping it with `useAsyncData`, the fetch will be executed on both the server and the client side. In Next.js, you can use `fetch` directly within Server Components to avoid client-side re-fetching.

In summary, both frameworks provide ways to fetch data on the server, but they differ in their caching and revalidation capabilities, server-side rendering emulation, and state transfer mechanisms.
